om  source code using one of the open source  build packs we support the build pack  will automatically detect the language  you're using and build the container for  you  cloudron also works with Cloud deploy so  you can set up deployment pipelines with  approval steps to manage the requirement  of services into production  car run has two main resources services  and jobs services are request-based  containers used most often for things  like external or internal web  applications apis or microservices they  can use HTTP HTTP 2 grpc websockets and  can even be treated asynchronously with  events  one of the great features of services is  traffic splitting and this allows you to  gradually roll out changes to a service  or quickly roll them back if something  goes wrong  so you just set a percentage like five  percent and the new version of your  service will only receive five percent  of traffic whereas the previous version  is still receiving 95 and then you just  gradually increase that and if anything  goes wrong you just set it to zero  and of course Services Auto scale so  when there's no traffic on the service  it'll scale down to zero and then when  traffic arrives it will automatically  scale up to meet demand  the other resource is jobs jobs are  great for background data processing  workloads so you might have a batch  process you want to run nightly maybe  you're cleaning up some data or  generating some reports you can schedule  jobs to run at a given date and time uh  you can trigger them manually or you can  trigger them via an API call  and jobs have an inbuilt retry mechanism  which improves the reliability of task  completion  now I want to just quickly touch on some  of the common use cases for cloud run uh  the first and probably most common is a  scalable web application now Cloud run  is a regional product which means that  within a region we provide zonal  redundancy regions in Google Cloud are  made up of several zones or cells and by  defau